title: collect & synthesize data
pid: 142b6f64-207e-4bb6-8ca3-a2a0b615e293
body: |2-
  Play the monome, across the whole space,
  and vary my finger velocity a lot. Then I'd "normalize" the data:
  For every key (x,y), collect all the waveforms that happened when I pressed it,
  and assume that they roughly cover the full range of impact velocities.

  But that's clearly imperfect, because my lowest velocity for one key
  might be lower than my lowest for another key.
  So I'd like to impose some kind of smoothing condition, whereby
  one key's can inform the keys of its neighbors.
  There will only be one contact mic per monome,
  plus the "control" mic mounted to something else.
  Each monome mic will presumably
  respond more to impacts in some regions than others,
  but in a small x-y neighborhood it probably responds roughly smiliarly.

  It might be ideal to simply gather a lot of data
  with the drummer playing in the same room, but that's not feasible.
  To make synthetic data, the obvious technique would be to replace
  the monome data with the some of some monome data and a drum recording,
  and put the drum recording alone on the control channel.
  But in reality those signals will be off slightly --
  maybe a foot or two of distance between them,
  so up to two miliseconds of lead or lag depending on how the mics
  are arranged relative to the speaker and the physical drums,
  plus, probably, subtle phase differences across each frequency.
  Changing the offset of the two signals is easy,
  but how to realistically mangling the drumkit signal
  to behave like it would in two different mics is not obvious to me.
